course: adult-life
lesson: 22
title: Sore backs, glasses and feeling low
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: As grown-ups get older, their bodies need more looking after: backs ache, eyes struggle with small print, ears fade, and teeth need regular checks.
explain: Minds need looking after too. Mental health is how well our thoughts and feelings are working, and sometimes it goes wrong for a while, just like a body can.
explain: Picture a café in Dublin: Mum holds the menu at arm's length and laughs, "I can't read this without my glasses," while Dad groans getting up from his chair, "It's my bad back again."
explain: Later that week, Dad says quietly that Mum has been low for weeks and the doctor says it is depression. She is seeing someone to talk to and is off work for a while.
explain: None of this is anybody's fault, none of it is catching, and most of it gets better with rest, help and time.
fact: A "bad back" usually means pain in the lower back from strained muscles or worn joints; it is very common in adults and can come and go for years.
fact: After about forty, the lens of the eye gets stiffer and close-up reading becomes harder, so many grown-ups need reading glasses.
fact: A hearing aid is a tiny device that makes sounds louder for someone whose hearing has faded; it whistles if it is not fitted right.
fact: To help someone with poor hearing, face them and speak clearly, without shouting.
fact: Dentists check teeth every six months to a year to catch small problems, like holes, before they hurt.
fact: A filling repairs a hole in a tooth; the dentist numbs the area first, and the numbness lasts a couple of hours, so talking and eating are hard.
fact: Mental health is how well our minds and feelings are working; everyone has it, and when someone is struggling it is nobody's fault.
fact: Depression is an illness where a person feels sad, empty or tired for weeks, and things they enjoyed stop feeling good; it is more than a bad day.
fact: Depression is treated with talking therapy, medicine or both, and it usually lifts; a child did not cause it and cannot fix it alone.
fact: Anxiety is worry or fear, often with a fast heartbeat; everyone has some, but for some people it grows big enough to get in the way.
fact: A counsellor is a trained person you talk to about problems and feelings; they listen, keep things private and help you find ways to cope.
fact: Burnout is exhaustion from too much stress for too long, usually from work; the person feels drained and cannot concentrate.
fact: A sick note, officially a fit note in the UK, is a doctor's letter saying a person is too unwell to work.
fact: "Signed off" means a doctor has said someone cannot work for now, because of a physical or a mental illness; it is part of getting better, not laziness.
exercise: Dad groans getting out of a chair: "It's my bad back again." What does he most likely mean? | His lower back hurts from strained muscles or worn joints | He has broken his spine in two places and needs an ambulance at once | He is pretending so he does not have to wash up | He has a rash on his back from a new shirt
exercise: What is a good way to help someone with a bad back? | Carry the heavy bag for them and do not jump on them | Jump on their back to push the bones into place | Make them run around the garden to loosen it | Tell them it is all in their head and ignore it
exercise: Mum holds the menu at arm's length: "I can't read this without my glasses." Why is this common in grown-ups? | After about forty the eye's lens stiffens and small print gets harder | Grown-ups read too many menus and wear out their eyes | Her eyes are getting longer because she is still growing, like a child does | Restaurants print their menus smaller for adults
exercise: Grandpa's hearing aid whistles and he says, "Pardon?" What is a hearing aid? | A tiny device that makes sounds louder for someone who hears poorly | A radio that plays music only he can hear | A device that stops all the noise around him so he can sleep at night in peace | A phone that hangs on the side of his ear
exercise: What is the best way to talk to Grandpa when he cannot hear well? | Face him and speak clearly without shouting | Shout as loudly as you can right into his ear | Whisper so that you do not hurt his ears | Give up halfway and say it does not matter
exercise: "Time for your check-up at the dentist." Why go when nothing hurts? | To catch small problems before they start to hurt | Because the dentist pulls a tooth out every visit | To have your teeth painted white for school photos | Because teeth only hurt after you see a dentist
exercise: After the dentist, Mum says, "She's had a filling, so her mouth is numb." Why is her mouth numb? | The dentist numbed it with an injection before fixing the hole | The filling is made of ice that freezes the mouth | She bit her tongue very hard in the dentist's chair | Fillings always make your mouth numb for a whole week, sometimes even longer
exercise: Your sister's mouth is still numb after a filling. What should she do? | Wait until the feeling comes back before eating | Eat a big crunchy apple to wake her mouth up | Drink something very hot to melt the numbness | Bite her cheek to check if it still hurts
exercise: "He's struggling with his mental health at the moment." What does it mean? | His mind and feelings are not working well for a while | He is finding his maths homework far too difficult this term | He has hurt his head and needs a bandage | He is pretending to be ill to get attention
exercise: "Mum's been low for weeks. The doctor says it's depression." What is depression? | An illness where someone feels sad, empty or tired for weeks | A bad mood that always goes away by the next morning, after a good sleep | Being cross because the children are noisy | Laziness that can be fixed by trying harder
exercise: Mum has depression. Whose fault is it? | Nobody's, it is an illness | The children's, for being naughty | Mum's, for not cheering up | Dad's, for working too much
exercise: Dad says, "I get anxious about driving on the motorway." What is anxiety? | A strong feeling of worry or fear, often with a fast heartbeat | A rule that some drivers must stay off motorways | A tiredness that comes from driving for far too long without a break | Anger at other drivers who go too fast
exercise: "Dad sees a counsellor on Thursdays." What does a counsellor do? | Listens, keeps things private and helps him cope | Gives him jobs to do for the town council at weekends | Checks his car is safe for the motorway | Decides whether he can keep his job or not
exercise: "She's off work with burnout." What is burnout? | Exhaustion from too much stress for too long | A burn from the oven at her work canteen at lunchtime | A holiday that her boss has given her | Being fired for working too slowly
exercise: "I've got a sick note for two weeks." What is a sick note? | A doctor's letter saying someone is too unwell to work | A note from a teacher saying you were naughty in class today | A card that friends send when you are ill | A list of medicines to buy at the chemist
exercise: "He's been signed off work." What does it mean? | A doctor has said he cannot work for now because he is ill | He has signed a new contract for a better job | He has been sacked and told never to come back, not even for his coat | He has signed his name to go on a long holiday
exercise: Is someone who is signed off with depression being lazy? | No, rest is part of getting better | Yes, they just do not want to work | Yes, only a broken bone counts as ill | No, they are on a secret holiday
test: Dad on the phone: "Can't come to five-a-side, mate, back's gone again." What happened? | His back is hurting again, so he cannot play | His team has gone away to the match without him | He has gone back home to fetch his boots | His friend's back has gone into hospital
test: Mum, patting her pockets: "Where are my bloody glasses?" They are on top of her head. What do you say? | "They're on your head, Mum!" | "You don't need glasses to see, Mum." | "Glasses are only for old people." | "You must have lost them for ever."
test: Your brother says, "Grandad's hearing aid whistles 'cause it's broke." How do you put him right? | It often whistles when it is not fitted right in the ear | Hearing aids whistle to tell you the time every hour | It whistles because Grandad is pretending | It whistles to warn that he is very ill
test: Gran grumbles, "Six months already? I only just went to the dentist." Why do dentists want you back so often? | Regular checks find small problems early | Dentists need to count your teeth for the council | Teeth fall out unless the dentist checks them | It is a rule that you must buy a toothbrush there
test: Your friend says, "My mum's depressed, it's my fault 'cause I'm always messing about." What is true? | It is not his fault, depression is an illness | He is right, children cause depression by being naughty | It will go away if he behaves for one day | He should stop talking to his mum
test: Two neighbours gossip: "He's off with stress again, bone idle that one." Why is this unfair? | Stress and burnout are real reasons to need rest | It is fair, stress is never a good reason to stop working | It is unfair because he is just on holiday | It is unfair because neighbours are never right
test: Dad mutters, "Taking the long way round, I'm not doing the motorway, it does my head in." Why might he avoid it? | He feels anxious about driving on the motorway | The motorway is closed to all cars on Sundays | He wants the trip to take longer for fun | His car is not allowed on the motorway
test: Your cousin whispers, "Uncle sees a shrink, he must be mad." How do you put her right? | Seeing a counsellor is a normal way to look after the mind | Only very dangerous people talk to counsellors, so she is right | Counsellors are doctors who fix broken legs | She is right and you should keep away from him
test: Mum on the phone: "The GP's signed me off for a fortnight, I'm running on empty." What does this tell you? | A doctor has said she must rest from work for two weeks | She has run out of petrol on the way to work | She has signed up to run a race in a fortnight with her friends from work | She has left her job and will start a new one
test: Your sister says, "Dad's got a sick note, so he's on holiday, let's go to the beach!" What is wrong? | Someone off sick needs quiet and rest, not a trip | Nothing, a sick note means a paid holiday for the family | Sick notes are only given to children | The beach is the best treatment for every illness
test: Grandma jokes, "My mouth feels like it belongs to someone else, love." Where has she been? | At the dentist, having a filling with a numbing injection | At the hairdresser, having her hair cut | At the optician, having her eyes tested | At the bank, opening a new account
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises depression? | An illness involving persistent low mood for weeks, ordinarily treatable | A transient bad mood that invariably resolves within a single day of rest | A deliberate choice on the part of the individual to avoid effort | A condition caused principally by the misconduct of children
exam: Identify the principal reason for which many persons above the age of forty require reading spectacles. | The lens of the eye becomes less flexible, impairing close vision | The eye ceases entirely to function in conditions of daylight | Printed text is customarily produced in smaller sizes for adults | Excessive reading in youth causes the eyes to shrink
exam: It is given that an employee has been "signed off" by a medical practitioner. Ascertain the meaning of this expression. | A doctor has certified that the person is presently unfit for work | The employee has signed a contract for new employment with another firm | The employee has been dismissed for misconduct | The employee has voluntarily commenced a holiday
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the function of a counsellor. | A trained person who listens in confidence and assists in coping | An official who determines whether a person may retain employment | A medical specialist who performs operations on the brain | A member of the local council who resolves disputes between neighbours
